{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Arthur awoke to the sensation of rough stone beneath his cheek. Disoriented, he sat up, blinking against the harsh sunlight filtering through a crack in a crumbling wall. The air smelled of damp earth and something faintly metallic. He glanced around, taking in the scene: a medieval village, complete with thatched-roof houses, a muddy marketplace, and people dressed in tunics and leather jerkins bustling about. But something felt…off. The way the sunlight seemed to bend and refract in unnatural ways, the perfect symmetry of the cobblestone streets, the almost too-realistic expressions on people's faces. He reached out to touch a nearby wooden cart, and it felt…solid, yet strangely responsive to his touch. A flicker of code danced across his vision, a brief, fleeting glimpse of lines of green text before vanishing as quickly as it appeared. He realized, with a chilling certainty, that he wasn't in Kansas anymore, or even in reality. He was inside a video game.\n\n***\n\nPanic clawed at his throat. He tried to call out, but his voice sounded strangely muffled, almost synthesized. He looked down at his hands – calloused and strong, but undeniably *human*, yet possessing a subtle, almost uncanny perfection. He felt an overwhelming urge to test the boundaries, to see how far he could push this fabricated world. A nearby villager bumped into him, muttering a greeting in a language he somehow understood, yet didn't recognize. The interaction felt pre-programmed, predictable. He started walking, cautiously exploring the village, searching for any clue, any glitch, any sign of the game's mechanics. Every detail felt meticulously crafted, every interaction flawlessly scripted. He was a character in someone else's world, and the realization was both terrifying and strangely exhilarating. His adventure had just begun.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Arthur", "logpr